In general relativity, an evolving black hole's boundary is intuitively a hypersurface in spacetime that grows as matter and radiation fall in. The classical notion of an event horizon is global and teleological, requiring knowledge of the entire future of spacetime, and is therefore unsuitable for many local and dynamical considerations. Several quasi-local proposals exist, including trapping horizons, dynamical horizons (smooth spacelike hypersurfaces foliated by marginally trapped 2-surfaces on which one null expansion vanishes and the other is strictly negative), future outer trapping horizons, and various marginally outer trapped tube constructions. However, these notions are not unique: the foliation by marginally trapped surfaces can fail to be unique, the hypersurface need not be everywhere spacelike under generic dynamics, jumps and bifurcations can occur, and existence/regularity in nonsymmetric spacetimes is not fully understood.

Problem. Formulate a mathematically precise definition of a "dynamical horizon" — a quasi-local hypersurface representing the moving boundary of an evolving black hole in a 4-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime (M, g) satisfying the Einstein equations with physically reasonable matter — that simultaneously:
(i) is purely quasi-local (depends only on a neighborhood of the hypersurface, not on global causal structure or asymptotic flatness);
(ii) reduces to a Killing/isolated horizon in equilibrium;
(iii) admits a unique, canonical foliation by closed marginally trapped 2-surfaces (so that area, angular momentum, and flux laws are unambiguously defined);
(iv) exists and is sufficiently regular for generic dynamical evolutions (e.g., gravitational collapse and binary mergers), with controlled behavior under bifurcations and jumps;
(v) yields a consistent first law and flux formulae for energy and angular momentum carried across the horizon.
Exhibit such a definition and prove its existence, uniqueness (up to a specified equivalence), and regularity, or prove that no definition meeting all of (i)–(v) exists.

